  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \n\nIn the mid/upper-levels, an Omega Block remains anchored across the \nLower 48, with high pressure expected to continue dominating the \nforecast across the CWA for the next 7 days. The apex of the \nsubtropical ridge, which is currently centered across the Great \nPlains, will gradually shift eastward over the next couple of days, \nas a high-amplitude, positively-tilted trough digs into the Desert \nSouthwest. The superposition of the subtropical ridge relative to \nthe CWA will maintain convergent flow aloft, keeping the formation \nof even diurnally-driven boundary-layer cu from occurring each \nafternoon. Excellent radiational cooling at night will result in the \nusual development of fog across the river valleys, with visibilities \nfalling to one-quarter mile at times; and fog of any kind will erode \nby 14-15Z each morning through the weekend. Some slight reductions \nwere made to the dewpoints for the forthcoming afternoons due to the \nfull insolation, along with bias-corrected tuning of the overnight \nlows tonight and Friday night due to the arrival of a modifying mP \nairmass trailing the decayed front/surface trough that is expected \npropagate through the region later this afternoon and tonight. The \narrival of this surface boundary should keep the development of \nfrost during the nighttime hours in check; however, patchy frost \ncannot be ruled out across the higher terrain tonight and beyond.",
